    "turn_text": "overall yes and i couldn't agree more look this is why i thought so hard for the family is why i thought so hard for the green line that's why i thought so hard for the silver line and the extension of the silver line we've done it several times now since i've been an office And I think all of those things are necessary. I've had real concerns over some people that think that Uber and Lyft are the final answer to everything. And again, they're fine. I use their services on occasion, but they are not the final answer to daily commuting for most people. And all that being said, I really would love to see this state jump to the fore on alternative methods of transportation. Obviously we have people not talking about a gondolas in downtown Boston. I don't know if that's going to help or not, but it's really worth looking at. I had a meeting yesterday with a city council from Chelsea who has an idea of taking the Tobin Bridge and adding something underneath it for a bikeway and a walkway. I think that's a great idea that needs to be reviewed. And again, there are many other people, the ideas that I would never be able to come up with. And I think every one of them has to be fully and totally explored. In Cambridge, there's discussion on what's called the Grand Junction line. It's a small, little single track line, putting some sort of a bikeway and a commuter pathway to move people from Austin over to Leechmere. Again, there are dozens of these things on the table. And then on top of that, we absolutely, positively must take our system that we have now and make it the best in the world. I know it's a big investment. but without that investment it's going to hurt our economy in the short-term in in the long-term",
